{
  "abstract": "We test direct copy-stacking (G_stack; Du et al., 2024) in a new recipe: a Muon + AdamW model trained on a warmup-stable-decay schedule whose linear warmdown over the last 50% of updates runs toward zero LR. A width-512, 4-layer trunk trains on the 8-layer schedule's clock. At update G it is grown to 8 layers by copying the layer stack in order, with its optimizer state, and training continues on the same token stream. Over 4 seeds the grown model at G = 190 (of 953 updates) beats an interpolated scratch 8-layer comparator matched on steady training time by −.0044 BPB (paired 95% t interval [−.0085, −.0002]) at .92 of the full 953-update scratch reference's steady time. At G = 286 the gain is −.0054 [−.0096, −.0012] at .87 of the time. Both exclude zero; neither establishes a .002 margin. At equal tokens it is +.0007 [−.0038, +.0052] behind the reference at G = 190: consistent with a tie, but not establishing one within .002. At G = 286 it is +.0038 behind. Growing later saves time but does not establish a better time-matched margin (paired shift −.0011 [−.0021, +.0000]). The bundled copy operator beats the tested zero-output controls: copying is uphill at insertion (+.20 nats), yet on seed 44 it ends .0184 BPB ahead of zero-output new layers at equal time and tokens. Four preregistered mechanism accounts failed (LR-proportional drift, a grown-in residual, a lag-to-endpoint proxy, G-scaled lag). Grown minus reference varies more across seeds (sd .0028) than the 880-update scratch run minus reference (sd .00014). The claim is limited to this schedule, one depth transition (4 to 8 layers at width 512), one data order shared by all seeds, and steady training time. On occupied wall-clock time, the seed-44 edge does not survive.",
